  "final_text": "Both objects now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Data exploration.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`, time field `timestamp`) holds web-server access logs. The useful fields are `response` (string, with `response.keyword` for exact matching), `clientip`, `bytes`, `url`, `extension`, `geo.dest`, `agent`, and `machine.os`.\n\n**Dashboard: `EFF-2d328fd0 Web logs overview`**\n- Link: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2d328fd0-web-logs-overview-fe8571253081\n- Five panels, default time range last 1 year:\n  - Total requests (count)\n  - Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n  - Average response size in bytes (average of `bytes`)\n  - Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n  - Requests by response code (bar, top 10 values of `response.keyword`)\n\n**Alert rule: `EFF-2d328fd0 5xx spike`**\n- ID `254ba07a-2b53-4fbe-a335-52f60b9b4426`, type `.es-query`, consumer `stackAlerts`.\n- Runs every 1m. Counts documents in the last 5 minutes whose `response.keyword` matches the wildcard `5??`, meaning any three-character code starting with 5. It fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Tagged `EFF-2d328fd0`. It has no action connectors, so it records alerts but does not notify anyone.\n\n**Choices I made that you may want to change:**\n- I enabled the rule. The create default is disabled, but a rule that never runs does not check anything. Disable it with `disable_alert_rule` if you want it off.\n- The 5xx filter uses the wildcard `5??`, not a numeric range, because `response` is a string field.\n- The dashboard time range is `now-1y`. I did not check the sample data's timestamp span, so if the panels look empty, widen the range.\n\nI did not run the rule's query against the data to confirm the 5xx count. The Kibana ES|QL endpoints I tried were not available on this instance, so the rule's status is `ok` but I have not seen it evaluate real hits.",
